{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>For your seed shall be strangers.</b> From the birth of Yitzchak until Israel went out of Egypt — four hundred years. How? Yitzchak was sixty years old when Yaakov was born, and Yaakov, when he went down to Egypt, said, ״יְמֵי שְׁנֵי מְגוּרַי שְׁלֹשִׁים וּמְאַת שָׁנָה״ — that is 130. And in Egypt they were two hundred and ten, by the count of רְדוּ — that is four hundred years. And if you would say they were in Egypt four hundred — behold, Kehat was among those who went down to Egypt. Go and count the years of Kehat and of Amram and the eighty of Moshe, who was when Israel went out of Egypt — you find no more than three hundred and fifty. And you must subtract from them all the years that Kehat lived after the birth of Amram and that Amram lived after the birth of Moshe." "<b>In a land not theirs.</b> It was not said 'in the land of Egypt,' but 'not theirs' — and from the time Yitzchak was born: ״וַיָּגָר אַבְרָהָם״ (Genesis 21), and regarding Yitzchak: ״גוּר בָּאָרֶץ הַזֹּאת״ (ibid. 26), and Yaakov: ״גָּר בְּאֶרֶץ חָם״ (Psalms 105), ״לָגוּר בָּאָרֶץ בָּאנוּ״ (Genesis 47)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:58:50.021552531Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}